---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
import ToastExamples from "../../components/demos/ToastExamples";
import ApiTable from "../../components/ApiTable.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Toast" withSidebar>
  <DocsSidebar slot="sidebar" />
  <div class="docs-prose">
    <h1>Toast</h1>
    <p>
      An imperative API, like Sonner: call <code>toast(...)</code> from
      anywhere in your code, no context provider needed at the call site.
      Mount <code>&lt;ToastViewport /&gt;</code> once, near the root of
      your app, to actually render them. Every toast is its own live
      region, <code>role="alert"</code> for <code>danger</code>/
      <code>warning</code>, <code>role="status"</code> for{" "}
      <code>default</code>/<code>success</code>, the same reasoning as{" "}
      <a href="/components/callout/">Callout</a>.
    </p>

    <ToastExamples client:visible />

    <h2>API</h2>
    <div class="docs-table-scroll"><table class="docs-table">
      <caption>The <code>toast</code> function.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>toast(title, options?)</code></td><td>Default variant</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>toast.success(title, options?)</code></td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>toast.warning(title, options?)</code></td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>toast.danger(title, options?)</code></td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>toast.dismiss(id)</code></td><td>Dismiss a specific toast early</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      <code>options</code>: <code>&#123; description?, duration? &#125;</code>. <code>duration</code> defaults to 4000ms; pass <code>0</code> to require a manual dismiss.
    </p>

    <h2>Swipe to dismiss</h2>
    <p>
      Drag a toast toward the edge of the screen it's anchored to (right in a
      left-to-right page) and it follows the pointer; drag it the other way and
      it resists, compressed asymptotically so it answers without pretending
      that direction does anything. Let go and it leaves if it travelled 40% of
      its width <em>or</em> was still moving faster than 0.5px/ms — so a short
      flick works as well as a long drag — and otherwise eases back. A pointer
      pulling back at the moment it lifts keeps the toast even past the
      distance line: it's intent that decides, not where the toast happens to
      sit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Speed is measured over the last 100ms of the gesture, not averaged across
      all of it, which is why the bar is 0.5 rather than the 0.11 you'll see
      in libraries that average: a drag that parks before release shouldn't
      read as a flick. It's the same measure and threshold as{" "}
      <a href="/components/sheet/">Sheet</a>, so one throw works on both.
    </p>
    <p>
      The gesture stays out of the way. A press on the close button, or any
      link or control you put inside a toast, is a click, never a swipe. Nothing
      is claimed until the pointer has moved 4px, mostly sideways, so a click is
      still a click and a vertical pan still scrolls the page; only then is the
      pointer captured. Under reduced motion the toast doesn't slide under the
      pointer — a swipe still dismisses it, by a fade. The close button remains
      the accessible way to dismiss; the swipe is a shortcut.
    </p>

    <h2>Props</h2>
    <ApiTable slug="toast" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
